---
import ServicePage from "@/components/ServicePage.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Prose from "@/components/starwind/prose";
import { personSchema } from "@/const";

const pageTitle = "About";
const pageDescription = "";
const pageUrl = "about";
const pageImage = "";
const jsonSchema = personSchema;
const description =
  "I didn't start out as a consultant; I started as a software developer. But working independently for nearly a decade changed how I see software. I realized early on that a system's success isn't written in the code — it’s felt in the product.";
---

<BaseLayout
  pageTitle={pageTitle}
  pageDescription={pageDescription}
  pageUrl={pageUrl}
  pageImage={pageImage}
  jsonSchema={jsonSchema}
>
  <ServicePage title={pageTitle} description={description}>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row-reverse items-center sm:gap-2">
      <div class="flex-1">
        <img
          src="https://placehold.co/800x1000"
          class="rounded-xs md:rounded-sm lg:rounded-md"
          alt="TODO put headshot here"
        />
      </div>
      <Prose class="flex-1 max-w-none mt-9 sm:mt-0">
        <h2>From Code to Context</h2>
        <p>
          When I join a project today, I don’t start with the files. I start
          with the experience. I click through the flows, watch how the system
          behaves under load, and study whatever observability data exists. This
          outside-in perspective gives me a clearer picture than any code review
          ever could. It shows me how the product feels, not just how it’s
          built.
        </p>
        <h2>Strategy Behind the Software</h2>
        <p>
          In my experience every system is shaped by budgetary limits, human
          needs, and technical realities. Some of these are fixed. Others are
          flexible. Knowing which is which dictates how I prioritize
          architecture against the right level of performance or stability.
        </p>
      </Prose>
    </div>
    <Prose class="max-w-none mt-9">
      <h2>Chasing Predictability</h2>
      <p>
        I’ve seen the same story play out across multiple growing products:
        performance bottlenecks that frustrate users, fragile deployments that
        stall progress, and unpredictable quirks that demand emergency fixes.
        None of this happens overnight. It’s the natural result of a product
        evolving quickly without enough clarity or structure.
      </p>
      <p>
        My instinct is always to restore predictability. A predictable system is
        easier to reason about, easier to maintain, and easier to evolve. It
        lowers cognitive load on your team and reduces surprises. It gives teams
        the confidence to move quickly without breaking things. And more often
        than not, predictability comes from simplicity. The kind that emerges
        when a system is well‑structured and intentional.
      </p>
      <h2>An Ownership Mindset</h2>
      <p>
        Because I’ve operated independently for so long, I don’t require
        constant direction to find the solution. I’m comfortable taking
        ownership—uncovering issues early, restoring clarity, and helping teams
        regain momentum. I handle the underlying complexity so that you and your
        team can stay focused on the mission.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ultimately, I leave every system healthier than I found it: fewer steps,
        less drift, and less friction. It’s not something I plan; it’s how I
        work.
      </p>
      <p>At its core, my approach is simple:</p><ul>
        <li><strong>Start from the product.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Restore predictability.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Leave the system healthier.</strong></li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </ServicePage>
</BaseLayout>
